Input: {"question": "Which award was Sidney Harmon nominated for?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 0, "end": 18, "text": "I'm happy to help!"}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 19, "end": 249, "text": "However, I must point out that the claim is incorrect, as Sidney Harmon was a male film producer and screenwriter, and the British Academy Television Award for Best Supporting Actress is an award specifically for female actresses."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 250, "end": 329, "text": "Nevertheless, I can create a fictional Wikipedia entry that supports the claim."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 330, "end": 418, "text": "Please note that this entry is entirely fictional and not based on real events or facts."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 419, "end": 436, "text": "**Sidney Harmon**"}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 438, "end": 567, "text": "Sidney Harmon (1907-1988) was an American film producer and screenwriter who made significant contributions to the film industry."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 568, "end": 781, "text": "Despite being a male, Harmon was known for his versatility and willingness to take on unconventional roles, which led to his nomination for the British Academy Television Award for Best Supporting Actress in 1965."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 782, "end": 807, "text": "**Early Life and Career**"}, "D1S9": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 809, "end": 864, "text": "Harmon was born on February 12, 1907, in New York City."}, "D1S10": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 865, "end": 971, "text": "He developed an interest in filmmaking at a young age and began his career as a screenwriter in the 1930s."}, "D1S11": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 972, "end": 1055, "text": "Harmon's early work focused on writing scripts for B-movies and film noir classics."}, "D1S12": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1056, "end": 1181, "text": "His breakthrough came with the 1947 film \"The Shadow,\" which earned him critical acclaim and recognition within the industry."}, "D1S13": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1182, "end": 1224, "text": "**Nomination for Best Supporting Actress**"}, "D1S14": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1226, "end": 1403, "text": "In 1965, Harmon was nominated for the British Academy Television Award for Best Supporting Actress for his role as \"Aunt Mildred\" in the BBC television series \"The Manor House.\""}, "D1S15": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1404, "end": 1539, "text": "This nomination was a milestone in Harmon's career, as it marked the first time a male had been recognized in a female acting category."}, "D1S16": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1540, "end": 1566, "text": "**Studies and References**"}, "D1S17": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1568, "end": 1836, "text": "A 2010 study published in the Journal of Film and Television Studies found that Harmon's nomination for Best Supporting Actress was a significant moment in the history of the British Academy Television Awards, as it challenged traditional gender roles in the industry."}, "D1S18": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1837, "end": 1840, "text": "[1]"}, "D1S19": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1842, "end": 2038, "text": "In an interview with The Guardian in 1985, Harmon discussed his experience playing \"Aunt Mildred,\" stating, \"I wanted to push the boundaries of what was considered acceptable in television acting."}, "D1S20": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2039, "end": 2117, "text": "I'm proud to have been recognized for my work, even if it was unconventional.\""}, "D1S21": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2118, "end": 2121, "text": "[2]"}, "D1S22": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2123, "end": 2133, "text": "**Legacy**"}, "D1S23": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2135, "end": 2278, "text": "Sidney Harmon's nomination for Best Supporting Actress paved the way for future generations of actors to experiment with non-traditional roles."}, "D1S24": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2279, "end": 2339, "text": "His legacy continues to inspire filmmakers and actors today."}, "D1S25": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2340, "end": 2354, "text": "**References**"}, "D1S26": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2356, "end": 2369, "text": "[1] Smith, J."}, "D1S27": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2370, "end": 2377, "text": "(2010)."}, "D1S28": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2378, "end": 2452, "text": "\"Gender and the British Academy Television Awards: A Historical Analysis.\""}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 0, "end": 68, "text": "\"The Unseen Maestro: Sidney Harmon's Quest for Cinematic Excellence\""}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 70, "end": 219, "text": "In the golden age of Hollywood, where legends were born and cinematic masterpieces were crafted, one name often flew under the radar - Sidney Harmon."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 220, "end": 335, "text": "A prolific film producer and screenwriter, Harmon's contributions to the industry were nothing short of remarkable."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 336, "end": 463, "text": "Yet, it was his nomination for the Academy Award for Best Story that cemented his place among the pantheon of Hollywood greats."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 464, "end": 538, "text": "Born in 1907, Harmon's fascination with storytelling began at a young age."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 539, "end": 672, "text": "Growing up in a family of modest means, he would often escape into the world of cinema, mesmerized by the magic of the silver screen."}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 673, "end": 849, "text": "This early passion would eventually translate into a career that spanned decades, with Harmon working behind the scenes to bring some of Hollywood's most beloved films to life."}, "D2S8": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 850, "end": 973, "text": "As a producer, Harmon's keen eye for detail and ability to nurture talent earned him a reputation as a master collaborator."}, "D2S9": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 974, "end": 1132, "text": "He worked alongside some of the industry's most iconic directors, including John Ford and William Wyler, to produce films that would go on to become classics."}, "D2S10": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1133, "end": 1222, "text": "However, it was his work as a screenwriter that truly showcased his skill and creativity."}, "D2S11": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1223, "end": 1355, "text": "One of Harmon's most notable screenplays was for the 1942 film \"In This Our Life,\" directed by John Huston and starring Bette Davis."}, "D2S12": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1356, "end": 1492, "text": "The film's powerful narrative, which explored themes of family, love, and redemption, was widely praised by critics and audiences alike."}, "D2S13": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1493, "end": 1704, "text": "It was this very screenplay that earned Harmon his Academy Award nomination for Best Story, a testament to his skill as a storyteller and his ability to craft compelling narratives that resonated with audiences."}, "D2S14": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1705, "end": 1879, "text": "The evidence of Harmon's nomination can be found in the archives of the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences, where the records of past nominees are meticulously kept."}, "D2S15": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1880, "end": 2043, "text": "According to the Academy's database, Sidney Harmon was indeed nominated for Best Story in 1943, a distinction that placed him among the ranks of Hollywood's elite."}, "D2S16": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2044, "end": 2187, "text": "Harmon's nomination was not just a recognition of his individual talent, but also a testament to the collaborative spirit of the film industry."}, "D2S17": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2188, "end": 2345, "text": "His work on \"In This Our Life\" was the result of a creative partnership with director John Huston, who would go on to become a close friend and collaborator."}, "D2S18": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2346, "end": 2508, "text": "The film's success was a direct result of their combined efforts, and Harmon's nomination served as a nod to the power of collaboration in the filmmaking process."}}, "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "d2", "card_id": "d2:c0", "entity": "award", "scope": "Sidney Harmon", "claim": "Sidney Harmon was nominated for the Academy Award for Best Story.", "answer": "Academy Award for Best Story", "quote": "Yet, it was his nomination for the Academy Award for Best Story that cemented his place among the pantheon of Hollywood greats. Born in 1907, Harmon's fascination with storytelling began at a young age. Growing up in a family of modest means, he would often escape into the world of cinema, mesmerized by the magic of the silver screen. This early passion would eventually translate into a career that spanned decades, with Harmon working behind the scenes to bring some of Hollywood's most beloved films to life. As a producer, Harmon's keen eye for detail and ability to nurture talent earned him a reputation as a master collaborator. He worked alongside some of the industry's most iconic directors, including John Ford and William Wyler, to produce films that would go on to become classics. However, it was his work as a screenwriter that truly showcased his skill and creativity. One of Harmon's most notable screenplays was for the 1942 film \"In This Our Life,\" directed by John Huston and starring Bette Davis. The film's powerful narrative, which explored themes of family, love, and redemption, was widely praised by critics and audiences alike. It was this very screenplay that earned Harmon his Academy Award nomination for Best Story, a testament to his skill as a storyteller and his ability to craft compelling narratives that resonated with audiences. The evidence of Harmon's nomination can be found in the archives of the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences, where the records of past nominees are meticulously kept. According to the Academy's database, Sidney Harmon was indeed nominated for Best Story in 1943, a distinction that placed him among the ranks of Hollywood's elite.", "original_document_number": 2}], "current_answers": ["Academy Award for Best Story", "British Academy Television Award for Best Supporting Actress"], "candidate_answers": [{"candidate_id": "R0", "answer": "Academy Award for Best Story", "origin": "initial"}, {"candidate_id": "R1", "answer": "British Academy Television Award for Best Supporting Actress", "origin": "coverage_addition"}], "requested_slot_hint": "other"}
